====================================================================================================
(1)  DATA-AVERAGED LINEAR LOSS:  does the v72 readout margin survive batching?
====================================================================================================
  4 samples on a 2-qubit register held in superposition and never
  uncomputed, so <O tensor I> is the batch mean. Same R and scoring as v72;
  SPSA charged 2 circuits per sigma sample (G=1 here) and given its best K.

   Nsys   M   T total   cos QLTO   cos SPSA   1-cos QL   1-cos SP   ratio   winner
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      4  24     32768     0.8842     0.7900    0.11577    0.20995    1.81     QLTO
      4  24    131072     0.9641     0.7968    0.03595    0.20317    5.65     QLTO
  ................................................................................
      6  36     32768     0.8622     0.8075    0.13783    0.19251    1.40     QLTO
      6  36    131072     0.9363     0.8106    0.06369    0.18940    2.97     QLTO
  ................................................................................

  v72 on a fixed observable reached 5.66x at N=4 and 5.21x at N=6 at its
  largest budget. A comparable ratio here means batching costs the readout
  nothing and QML sits on the same footing as the other applications.

====================================================================================================
(2)  NONLINEAR LOSS:  is the shared-readout direction usable for MSE?
====================================================================================================
  One shared readout returns grad of the linear surrogate mean_x f_x. The MSE
  gradient reweights sample x by (f_x - y_x). They are different vectors; the
  question is whether they point the same way, measured along a real descent
  trajectory since the weights move as f moves.

   epoch       MSE    |g_mse|    |g_lin|   cos(g_lin, g_mse)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
       0   0.35889    0.39262    0.26108             -0.7431
       3   0.23862    0.29590    0.24235             -0.0923
       6   0.15825    0.24345    0.26415              0.2981
       9   0.10892    0.18044    0.29782              0.2663

  A cosine near 1 means the shared readout is a usable descent direction for
  MSE and the primitive transfers with a bias caveat. Near 0 or negative means
  nonlinear losses need their own construction. Note the linear surrogate does
  not depend on the labels at all, so agreement is a property of the landscape
  and not of the task, and is not evidence that the method solves the
  supervised problem.
